  "brief": {
    "story": "One push button should light three LEDs through Q1. Each LED branch wants about 14 mA, so Q1 has to pass about 43 mA, and it only passes β × its base current (β ≈ 200).",
    "goal": "Fit Q1’s base resistor (column 7 to column 11) so all three LEDs carry 13–16 mA while SW1 is held, with the base current under 2 mA.",
    "datasheet": {
      "title": "BC547 · NPN",
      "rows": [
        [
          "Legs",
          "E · B · C"
        ],
        [
          "β (gain)",
          "about 200"
        ],
        [
          "Base on at",
          "0.7 V"
        ],
        [
          "Load",
          "3 × 14.5 mA ≈ 43 mA"
        ]
      ]
    }
  },
  "hints": [
    "Q1 can only pass β × I_B. For 43 mA it needs at least 43 mA ÷ 200 = 0.22 mA into the base.",
    "I_B = (9 V − 0.7 V) ÷ R_B, so R_B can be at most 8.3 V ÷ 0.22 mA ≈ 38 kΩ. Give it a margin: designers aim for 5–10 times more base current than the minimum.",
    "10 kΩ gives 0.83 mA (room for 166 mA). 47 kΩ is too little (35 mA shared by three). 1 kΩ breaks the 2 mA limit."
  ],
